sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/hygromorph-bilayer-scaling--claude-opus-5-5--2> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "FE correction factors for fibre angle, gradients and poroelastic lag" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/hygromorph-bilayer-scaling> ;
    dct:description "Quantify, before looking at data, how far real hygromorph features push curvature and response time from the Timoshenko and h^2/D predictions. Run FEniCSx models of strips with (a) misaligned swelling direction at angle phi (reducing bending and adding twist, consistent with the Lean-checked antisymmetric-twist theorems), (b) continuously graded modulus and eigenstrain through the thickness, (c) imperfect bonding via a compliant interlayer, (d) poroelastic coupling with layer-specific diffusivities, and (e) linear viscoelastic relaxation. Produce dimensionless correction maps kappa_FE/kappa_Timoshenko and tau_FE/(h^2/D) over the parameter ranges reported for pine cones, awns and printed bilayers. These maps convert approach 1 from a pass/fail of the ideal model into a test of whether measured deviations are explained by measured structure." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "Correction maps with the boundary of the 10% validity region of the ideal model for each departure, checked for mesh convergence." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Corrections that depend on unmeasured parameters (e.g. interlayer compliance) so strongly that any measured curvature can be fitted, making the corrected model unfalsifiable." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/simulation" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Verify the FE code against the Timoshenko formula (error below 1% for slender, perfectly bonded, uniform layers) and against the first diffusion mode decay rate pi^2 D/h^2. 2. Sweep each departure separately over literature ranges, then in pairs. 3. Publish correction tables and the parameter region where Timoshenko is within 10%. 4. Pre-register their use as the 'structure-corrected' model in approach 1." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: fenicsx" ;
    skos:note "Tool: python" ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch10-01> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1364/josa.11.000233> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1203874> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms2666> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/hygromorph-bilayer-scaling> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Do real hygromorphs follow bilayer curvature and diffusive response-time scaling?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1364/josa.11.000233> dct:title "Analysis of Bi-Metal Thermostats" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Timoshenko 1925, Analysis of bi-metal thermostats" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1203874> dct:title "Geometry and Mechanics in the Opening of Chiral Seed Pods" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Armon et al. 2011, chiral seed pods" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms2666> dct:title "Self-shaping composites with programmable bioinspired microstructures" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Erb et al. 2013, Self-shaping composites" .
}

